---
import { FAQSection, RelatedArticles } from "../../components/article";
import ComparisonTable from "../../components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { getArticleBySlug } from "../../data/articles";

// Get article metadata from central data file
const article = getArticleBySlug("accept-international-payments-freelancer", "tools")!;
const { title, description, readingTime, image, imageAlt } = article;
const pubDate = new Date(article.pubDate);

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "hidden-tax", text: 'The "Hidden Tax" on Your Work' },
  { depth: 2, slug: "best-ways", text: "The 3 Best Ways to Get Paid (Ranked)" },
  {
    depth: 2,
    slug: "money-test",
    text: "The Money Test: What You Actually Keep",
  },
  { depth: 2, slug: "other-options", text: "Other Options (The Niche Stuff)" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "faq", text: "FAQ: The Stuff You Actually Ask" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "final-verdict", text: "Final Verdict" },
];

const feeComparisonColumns = [
  { key: "platform", label: "Platform" },
  { key: "fee", label: "Fee" },
  { key: "rate", label: "Exchange Rate" },
  { key: "receive", label: "You keep (approx)" },
];

const feeComparisonRows = [
  {
    platform: "<strong>Wise</strong>",
    fee: "Free to receive EUR locally",
    rate: "Mid-market, fee about 0.5% or less",
    receive: "<strong>~99.5%</strong>",
  },
  {
    platform: "<strong>Payoneer</strong>",
    fee: "1% to receive EUR (not your local currency)",
    rate: "0.5% to 4% depending on how you convert/withdraw",
    receive: "~95-98%",
  },
  {
    platform: "<strong>PayPal</strong>",
    fee: "3.49% + 1.5% international + fixed fee",
    rate: "3% conversion spread",
    receive: "~92%",
  },
  {
    platform: "<strong>Bank Wire</strong>",
    fee: "$15-50 incoming, depends on your bank",
    rate: "Usually 3-5% markup",
    receive: "~94-96%",
  },
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: "Do I need a business bank account?",
    answer:
      'Technically, no. But you should get one. It keeps your <a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers/">taxes</a> clean. Wise acts as a business account for most freelancers. Check our <a href="/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/">banking guide</a> for more options.',
  },
  {
    question: "How do I invoice in a different currency?",
    answer:
      "Just change the symbol on your invoice template. If you have a Wise USD account, invoice in USD. If you have a Wise EUR account, invoice in EUR. Give the client the bank details that match the currency.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if the client refuses to use Wise?",
    answer:
      "Ask if they can do a bank transfer. If you give them your Wise local bank details (e. G., your US routing number), to them, it IS a regular bank transfer. They don't even need to know you are using Wise.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is this legal? What about taxes?",
    answer:
      'Yes, it is legal. Income is income. It doesn\'t matter if it lands in PayPal, Wise, or a shoebox. You report the value in your home currency at the time you received it. See our <a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers/">tax guide for remote workers</a> for more details. (I am not an accountant. Please talk to one.)',
  },
  {
    question: "Does my client pay any fees if I use Wise?",
    answer:
      "Not if they pay your Wise local bank details with a normal domestic transfer. For them it's just a regular payment. The one exception is on your side: a US client who sends a wire instead of ACH costs you $6.11. Ask for ACH.",
  },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Wise vs PayPal vs Payoneer",
    excerpt: "Detailed comparison of the top international payment platforms.",
    href: "/tools/wise-vs-paypal-vs-payoneer/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1563013544-824ae1b704d3-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Payment platform comparison",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Banking for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Find the right bank account for international freelancing.",
    href: "/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1601597111158-2fceff292cdc-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Digital banking on mobile device",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Invoicing Tools",
    excerpt: "Invoice international clients professionally.",
    href: "/tools/best-invoicing-tools/",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1450101499163-c8848c66ca85-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Invoice and payment documents",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="tools"
    subcategorySlug="finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    So I landed my first big international client. A design agency in London.
    $2,000 for a project. I was thrilled.
  </p>
  <p>I sent the invoice. They paid via PayPal.</p>
  <p>Then I looked at my balance.</p>
  <p>$1,912.</p>
  <p>Wait. Where did the other $88 go?</p>
  <p>
    PayPal took a cut. Then they gave me a terrible exchange rate on the Pounds
    to Dollars conversion.
  </p>
  <p>Eighty-eight bucks to access my own earnings. Absurd.</p>
  <p>Never again.</p>
  <p>
    If you're freelancing globally, you are bleeding money right now. Banks,
    PayPal, and old-school wire transfers are taking 3-5% of your income.
  </p>
  <p>You don't have to let them.</p>
  <p>
    Here is how you actually get paid, keep your money, and stop donating your
    hard-earned cash to financial institutions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hidden-tax">The "Hidden Tax" on Your Work</h2>
  <p>Here is the dirty secret: "No Fee" is a lie.</p>
  <p>
    When a bank says "0% Commission" on currency exchange, they are lying to
    you. They just hide the fee in the exchange rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google says 1 USD = 0.92 EUR.<br/>Your bank gives you 1 USD = 0.88 EUR.
  </p>
  <p>
    That 4% difference? That's the fee. They just didn't put it on a receipt.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you make $50,000 a year from international clients, you could be losing
    $2,000 to $3,000 just on bad exchange rates. That's a vacation. That's a new
    <a href="/gear/best-laptops-remote-work/">MacBook</a>. Track this using good <a
      href="/tools/best-accounting-software-freelancers/">accounting software</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Stop doing it.</p>

  <h2 id="best-ways">The 3 Best Ways to Get Paid (Ranked)</h2>

  <h3>1. Wise (Formerly TransferWise)</h3>
  <p>This is the one I use. (Want the long version? <a href="/tools/wise-vs-paypal-vs-payoneer/">Wise vs PayPal vs Payoneer</a>, compared head to head.) It is the only one I actually recommend to friends.</p>
  <p>Why? Because they don't lie about the exchange rate.</p>
  <p>If you Google "USD to GBP", that is the rate Wise gives you. They charge a small, upfront conversion fee. It starts at 0.23% and depends on the currency pair. For the big ones you're usually well under 1%, and Wise shows you the exact number before you convert.</p>

  <p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>You open a Wise account.</li>
    <li>You click "Open a balance" in USD, EUR, GBP, AUD, etc.</li>
    <li>Wise gives you REAL bank details for those countries.</li>
    <li>You put those details on your invoice.</li>
    <li>The client pays you like you are a local. It's free for them.</li>
    <li>You get the money. You convert it when you want.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It is fast, cheap, and honest.</p>

  <p>One small catch. Getting paid by local transfer is free in USD, EUR, GBP, CAD, AUD and a few others. But if a US client sends a wire instead of ACH, Wise charges $6.11 to receive it. So I'd put "ACH preferred" right on the invoice.</p>

  <h3>2. Payoneer</h3>
  <p>If you live on Upwork, Fiverr, or Amazon, you probably need Payoneer.</p>
  <p>It is built for platforms. It integrates with everything.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>The good:</strong> It is easy to get a debit card and spend your money.<br
  />
    <strong>The bad:</strong> It's a pile of small fees. Getting paid into a Payoneer receiving account in
    your own local currency is free. In any other currency it's 1%. Moving money between your Payoneer currency
    balances costs 0.5%, and withdrawing to a bank in a different currency runs 1.2% to 4% depending on the route. If
    a client pays by card, up to 3.99% plus 49 cents. It adds up.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a solid "Plan B" or a necessity if your platform forces you to use it.
  </p>

  <h3>3. PayPal</h3>
  <p>The dinosaur.</p>
  <p>Everyone has it. That is the only reason it is still on this list.</p>
  <p>
    If a client says "I only use PayPal," you use PayPal. You don't lose a
    client over a payment method.
  </p>
  <p>But be aware: You are paying for the privilege. PayPal's current US rates are 3.49% plus a fixed fee when a client pays your invoice with PayPal. Then another 1.5% because the client is in another country. Then, if the money changes currency, PayPal's conversion spread is 3%. Stack those up and you're close to 8% on a single international payment.</p>
  <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If a client insists on PayPal, add a processing fee to your invoice. Honestly 5% doesn't even cover it anymore. See if they suddenly become open to a bank transfer.</p>

  <h2 id="money-test">The Money Test: What You Actually Keep</h2>
  <p>
    Let's say a client in Europe owes you €1,000. You want it in your US bank
    account (USD).
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable
      columns={feeComparisonColumns}
      rows={feeComparisonRows}
      highlight={0}
  />

  <p>Look at the difference between Wise and PayPal. On €1,000 that's roughly €70-75 gone.</p>
  <p>Do that once a month and it's close to €900 a year.</p>

  <h2 id="other-options">Other Options (The Niche Stuff)</h2>

  <h3>Stripe</h3>
  <p>
    Great if you want to look professional and accept credit cards directly on
    your invoices.
  </p>
  <p>But you pay for it. 2.9% + 30¢ per domestic card payment, plus 1.5% for international cards, plus 1% more if the currency has to be converted.</p>
  <p>
    Use it if you sell digital products or need a "Buy Now" button. Don't use it
    for large freelance invoices.
  </p>

  <h3>Crypto (USDC / Bitcoin)</h3>
  <p>
    I have a few tech clients who pay in USDC (a stablecoin pegged to the
    dollar). Check our <a href="/blog/crypto-friendly-banks/">crypto-friendly banks guide</a> if you go this route.
  </p>
  <p>Fees are near zero. Speed is instant. And if you already use Stripe, it now accepts stablecoin payments for 1.5%, conversion to dollars included. That's actually the easiest way to say yes to a crypto client.</p>
  <p>
    But good luck explaining it to a non-tech client. "So I need to open a
    wallet..." No. They won't do it.
  </p>
  <p>Only use this if the client suggests it.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ: The Stuff You Actually Ask</h2>
  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2 id="final-verdict">Final Verdict</h2>
  <p>Stop overcomplicating it.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open a Wise account.</li>
    <li>Get your local bank details for USD, EUR, and GBP.</li>
    <li>Put those details on your <a href="/tools/best-invoicing-tools/">invoices</a>, next to clear <a href="/guides/invoice-payment-terms-guide/">payment terms</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Holding money in several currencies long term? That's a separate question, covered in <a
      href="/guides/multi-currency-banking-nomads/">multi-currency banking</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will save thousands of dollars over your career. It takes 10 minutes to
    set up. Do it.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
